# fortran-names -- the Fortran names Fortmacs gives to Lisp-derived terms.
#
# Fortmacs is Fortran-first: where upstream names a thing with a Lisp
# term, Fortmacs uses a Fortran word.  Every term, name and file entry
# below was confirmed by the project owner individually; do not add one
# without that.
# tools/fortran-names.sh applies this table (to this tree, to branches
# written before an entry existed, and to code freshly ported from a new
# upstream version), and tools/check.sh fails on what it would still
# rename (tools/fortran-names.awk).  PORTING.md, "Fortran names for Lisp
# terms", explains it.
#
# One entry per line, fields separated by one tab:
#
#   term<TAB>OLD<TAB>NEW
#       OLD as an underscore-delimited part of any identifier or as a
#       word of prose, and its plural OLD + "s" (NEW + "s"):
#       forward_sexp -> forward_expression, "sexps" -> "expressions".
#   name<TAB>OLD<TAB>NEW[<TAB>FILE...]
#       the identifier OLD, also as a run of underscore-delimited parts
#       of a longer one (cmd_forward_list -> cmd_forward_group).  Names
#       are tried before terms, the longest first.  With FILEs (paths
#       separated by spaces), the name applies in those files only, to an
#       identifier that starts with OLD, before any other entry for OLD.
#   file<TAB>OLD<TAB>NEW
#       a module: its files (src/OLD.f90, test/test_OLD.f90) are renamed
#       and OLD is a name; when both end in "_mod" the stems are names
#       too (init_lisp_cmds -> init_expression_motion).
#   keep<TAB>OLD[<TAB>FILE...]
#       an identifier left as it is although a name or term matches it
#       (in the FILEs only, paths separated by spaces, if any are given):
#       a Lisp-mode name, or one awaiting the owner's decision.
#   keep<TAB>PATH
#       a file left as it is (a path with a slash, from the top of the
#       repository): Lisp-language content, such as a Lisp mode's
#       keywords, or a tool that reads upstream's Lisp and C.
#   keep<TAB>"TEXT"
#       TEXT, wherever it occurs, left as it is: Lisp source in string
#       literals, such as a test's buffer text.  Only the words of
#       user-visible prose are renamed, never Lisp that code matches.
#   Every keep entry says why in a comment above it.
#
# Matching ignores case and keeps it: ERROR_REGEXP_ALIST becomes
# ERROR_REGEXP_TABLE, "Sexp" becomes "Expression".  Upstream names are
# never renamed: FILE.el:NAME, FILE.c:NAME and FILE.h:NAME citations,
# hyphenated names (forward-sexp), C names (Fscan_lists, Vfoo_alist),
# upper-case C predicate macros (CHAR_HEAD_P, for a name ending in p),
# the name right after "upstream" or "upstream's", lines that say
# "fortran-names: keep", and lines between "fortran-names: off" and
# "fortran-names: on".
#
# Copyright (C) 2026 The Fortmacs authors
#
# This file is part of Fortmacs, under the GNU GPL version 3 or later.

